        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Hoabinhian</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>: more than 120 sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, among them those of Hòa Bình province and Da But; Spirit Cave, Sai Yok, Tham Lod and Ban Rai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>; Pha Faen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>; Gua Cha in Malaysia; sites in Terengganu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, Laos, Myanmar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>; and Xiaodong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the oldest and the only one in China.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Usually dated c. 10,000–2000 BCE, from the late Pleistocene to the Holocene; the 1994 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> conference set it at the “late-to-terminal Pleistocene to early-to-mid Holocene”. Xiaodong dates from 41,500 BCE, Vietnamese finds to 17,000 years before the present, Spirit Cave’s Hoabinhian levels to 12,000–8000 BP; the Bacsonian variant c. 8000–4000 BCE.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">flaked cobble tools, often worked on one face only; sumatraliths, cobbles flaked all over one side; grinding stones, often found with iron oxide; rock shelters near streams in limestone uplands; shell middens; edge-ground tools and cord-marked pottery in the upper layers; plant remains at Spirit Cave</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Madeleine Colani’s excavations in Hòa Bình province, published in 1927, and the 1932 definition of the First Congress of Prehistorians of the Far East; J. M. Matthews’s 1964 study at Sai Yok; Chester Gorman’s excavations and radiocarbon dates at Spirit Cave; the 1994 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> conference; ancient DNA from Pha Faen and Gua Cha.</span></div>
