    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Hoabinhian is a tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a>, chiefly flaked river cobbles, left by hunter-gatherers across mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> from the late Pleistocene to the Holocene.</li><li>It was first defined from Madeleine Colani’s excavations in Hòa Bình province, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, but later study showed that its tools form a continuous range of shapes and sizes rather than distinct types.</li><li>Ancient DNA from two Hoabinhian individuals shows a deeply branching East Asian ancestry, shared most closely today with peoples such as the Andamanese Onge and Jarawa, the Semang and the Maniq.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Madeleine Colani sorted just 82 artefacts from Sao Dong into 28 types; her typology proved so complicated that most Hoabinhian sites are identified simply by the presence of sumatraliths.</p></aside>
    </section>

